i {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/country"} "Medals by country"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/athlete"} "Medals by athlete"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal_table"} "Medal tables"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/participation"} "Participations"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/records"} "Olympic Records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age"} "Age records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age_at_death"} "Age at death"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/lists"} "Lists"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/today"} "Today"]] "\n" [:li {}] "\n" [:li {:class "divider"}] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics"} "Statistics"]] "\n"] "\n"] "\n\n\n\n\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/feedbacks/new"} "Feedback"]] "\n" [:form {:class "navbar-form navbar-left", :role "search", :action "/athletes/quick_search", :accept-charset "UTF-8", :method "post"} [:input {:name "utf8", :type "hidden", :value "✓", :autocomplete "off"}] [:input {:type "hidden", :name "authenticity_token", :value "7KVpQDMVPLpbhvxpmON1EyIgLFN0cPB_3WK1bE-kTsbmWZ5PZEZmZBTDK_Os7K0LV2cABYb0x_famF0_BjNPdA", :autocomplete "off"}] [:div {:class "form-group"} "\n" [:input {:type "text", :name "query", :id "query", :class "form-control", :placeholder "Athlete search"}] "\n"] "\n" [:input {:type "submit", :name "commit", :value "Go", :class "btn btn-default", :data-disable-with "Go"}] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "container"} "\n" [:ol {:class "breadcrumb"} "\n" [:li {} "\n" [:a {:href "/"} "Home"] "\n"] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/editions"} "Games"]] [:li {:class "active"} "1936 Summer Olympics Overview"] "\n"] "\n\n\n" [:h1 {} "1936 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:h2 {} "Facts"] "\n" [:table {:class "biotable"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition type"] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic Games"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Number and Year"] "\n" [:td {} "XI / 1936"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Host city"] "\n" [:td {} "\nBerlin, Germany\n(" [:a {:href "/venues/editions/11"} "Venues"] ")\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Opening ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n 1 August\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Closing ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n16 August\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n1 – 16 August\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "OCOG"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/organizations/354"} "Organisationskomitee für die XI. Olympiade Berlin 1936"]] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/11"} "4483"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/11/countries"} "49 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n149\nin 27 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Other events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n2\nin 2 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Berlin had been promised the " [:a {:href "/editions/50"} "1916 Olympic Games"] ", which were never held because of World War I. Twenty years later, the Olympics came to Berlin, as the world hovered on the brink of another, even more destructive war."] "\n\n" [:p {} "There were many protests against the Olympics being held in Berlin in 1936. The Americans came the closest to boycotting in protest, although the British and French both considered the option. In the end, all the usual nations competed, with the exception of Spain, which was involved in its own Civil War, and which scheduled a competing sports festival, the People’s Olympiad, in Barcelona (although it was eventually cancelled)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Many of the protests dealt with Hitler’s and Germany’s treatment of Jews, which also included the very real possibility that all Jews would be excluded from German Olympic teams. In the end, Germany placed one token Jew on the team, fencer " [:a {:href "/athletes/20711"} "Helene Mayer"] ", to assuage the protestors. The most controversial exclusion was that of German high jumper Margarethe “Gretel” Bergmann, who had been in the top 20 in the world lists in 1934-35, and would end 1936 in the top 10. She never competed at the Olympics and later emigrated to the United States."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The USOC asked future " [:a {:href "/organizations/1"} "IOC"] " President " [:a {:href "/athletes/78168"} "Avery Brundage"] " to visit Germany, to discuss the concerns with Jews being excluded, and he returned with a report that spoke glowingly of the Germany sports programmes. He was, at the time, the President of the US Olympic Committee and opposed all US efforts to boycott Berlin. He was successful. One US member of the IOC, " [:a {:href "/athletes/899199"} "Ernest Lee Jahncke"] ", opposed competing at Berlin and, for his support of the boycott, he was eventually expelled from the IOC, to be replaced by Brundage."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the end, the 1936 Olympic Games were magnificently staged, as " [:a {:href "/athletes/1100010"} "Adolf Hitler"] " spared no expense and used them as a propaganda tool to demonstrate the beauty and efficiency of the Third Reich. He had " [:a {:href "/athletes/1200012"} "Leni Riefenstahl"] ", a renowned German filmmaker, produce a wondrous movie, " [:em {} "Olympia"] ", to ensure that the propaganda would not end at the Closing Ceremony. For Riefenstahl, her association with Hitler would basically end her career in film making."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The greatest innovation of the 1936 Olympics was conceived by Dr. " [:a {:href "/athletes/921849"} "Carl Diem"] ", head of the Organizing Committee. He proposed that a torch relay be instituted, to carry a flame from Ancient Olympia to the Berlin Stadium, and then to light the Olympic Flame at the stadium. On 20 July 1936, 15 Greek maidens clad in short, belted smocks, representing the robes of priestesses, gathered on the plain at Ancient Olympia and the flame was lit there, by the rays of the Greek sun off a reflector. The high priestess presented the flame to " [:a {:href "/athletes/1700535"} "Konstantinos Kondylis"] ", the first Greek runner, to begin a torch relay. After several thousand kilometres, the flame arrived in Berlin, where it was lit in the stadium by " [:a {:href "/athletes/1100066"} "Fritz Schilgen"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Several sports were held for the first time at Berlin, notably " [:a {:href "/results/31800"} "basketball"] ", " [:a {:href "/editions/11/sports/CSP"} "canoeing"] ", and " [:a {:href "/results/34500"} "handball"] ", although the latter was played as 11-a-side field handball, rather than the modern indoor 7-a-side game. Canoeing had been a " [:a {:href "/editions/8/sports/CSP"} "demonstration sport in 1924"] ". Polo was contested, after having been absent from the programme in " [:a {:href "/editions/9"} "1928"] " and " [:a {:href "/editions/10"} "1932"] ", but it would never be contested again at the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "On the sporting fields, the most significant performances came from Black American " [:a {:href "/athletes/78869"} "Jesse Owens"] ", who put the lie to Hitler’s theories of Aryan supremacy. The sprinter/long jumper won four gold medals, in the " [:a {:href "/results/58143"} "100 metres"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/58166"} "200 metres"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/58263"} "4x100 metre relay"] ", and " [:a {:href "/results/58318"} "long jump"] ". It would be 48 years before anyone would equal Owens’ feat in athletics, when " [:a {:href "/athletes/78692"} "Carl Lewis"] " won the same events at the 1984 Los Angeles Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Hitler once said he wanted all future Olympic Games to be held in Germany, and even envisioned a 400,000 seat Olympic stadium, but that would not occur, and the Games would not return to Germany until " [:a {:href "/editions/18"} "1972 and München"] ". The " [:a {:href "/editions/51"} "1940 Olympics"] " were scheduled for Tokyo, but neither those Games nor the " [:a {:href "/editions/52"} "1944 Olympics"] " would be held because of World War II. When one thinks of the Berlin Games, don’t remember Hitler and how he tried to control the 1936 Olympics – remember Jesse Owens and how he did."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "By 1930, no less than 14 cities had shown interest in hosting the 1936 Summer Olympics: Berlin (Germany), Barcelona (Spain), Alexandria (Egypt), Budapest (Hungary), Buenos Aires (Argentina), Dublin (Ireland), Köln (Cologne) (Germany), Dublin (Ireland), Frankfurt am Main (Germany), Helsinki (Finland), Lausanne (Switzerland), Nürnberg (Germany), Rio de Janeiro (Brazil), and Roma (Italy). By the time the 30th " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "IOC Session"] " in Barcelona was scheduled, all but two bids were withdrawn: Barcelona itself and Berlin. Because only 19 voting members were present at the IOC Session, a postal vote was held, which Berlin won 43 - 16."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Ceremonies"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped table-condensed"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/officially_opened"} "Officially opened by"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1100010"} "Adolf Hitler"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "Chancellor"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/torch_bearers"} "Torchbearer"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1700511"} "Koula Pratsika"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GRE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GRE.png"}] "GRE"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "High Priestess at Olympia Flame Lighting Ceremony"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {}] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1700535"} "Konstantinos Kondylis"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GRE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GRE.png"}] "GRE"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "First to